<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mpumalanga</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mpumalanga is a province of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, created in 1994 from the eastern part of the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> and given its present name in 1995. Its capital is Mbombela. It includes part of the Kruger National Park, rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> deposits and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> escarpment, and many of its people are Swazi-speaking.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mpumalanga.jpg" alt="Mpumalanga" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JMK · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R33_by_Assegaairivier,_Piet_Retief,_Mpumalanga,_a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name means East, or literally the place where the sun rises, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nguni">Nguni</a> languages. The province borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> to the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauteng">Gauteng</a> to the west, the Free State to the south-west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwazulu-natal">KwaZulu-Natal</a> to the south, and takes up 6.3 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s land. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> escarpment divides it into a western half of high-altitude grassland, the Highveld, and an eastern half of low-altitude subtropical Lowveld, mostly savanna, where the southern half of Kruger National Park lies. Some of the oldest rocks on earth have been found in the Barberton area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological sites show human settlement going back 1.7 million years, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngwenya-mine">Lion Cavern</a> at Ngwenya on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eswatini">Eswatini</a> border shows iron ore being mined at least 28,000 years ago. The Lydenburg Heads, from about 500 CE, are the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> artworks south of the equator. Stone-walled Mbayi sites on the Highveld point to a large agropastoral society between about 1500 and 1820, connected to the Portuguese trading post in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Maputo Bay</a>. The groups that became the Swazi, Pedi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsonga-people">Tsonga peoples</a> settled in the area.</p><p>The first Dutch-speaking settlers arrived in 1845 in the Great Trek, and in 1852 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> established the South African Republic. The Pedi, Swazi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> kingdoms offered significant resistance, and the Pedi fought the Boers in the Sekhukhune Wars of 1876. Gold was first found near Barberton in 1883, and the region became a thoroughfare for trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delagoa-bay">Delagoa Bay</a>. Under a system known as Inboekstelsel, Boers raided to capture children and force them into indentured labour. Kruger National Park was established in 1898.</p><p>After 1948 apartheid policy moved Black South Africans by forced removals into homelands, among them KaNgwane, KwaNdebele, Lebowa and Gazankulu. In 1959 the town of Bethal was the centre of a consumer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> boycott against conditions on local farms. In 1986 a disaster at Kinross Mine killed 177 mine workers. The province was formed on 27 April 1994, the day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s first democratic elections, from the Eastern Transvaal and the former homelands, and was renamed Mpumalanga on 24 August 1995.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is Mbombela. The province is home to Swazi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndebele-kingdom">Ndebele</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>, Tsonga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a>, Mapulana, Portuguese and Pedi communities, and the Ndebele Cultural Village at Botshabelo displays Ndebele house painting. At the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> it had about four million people, 30 percent of whom spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swazi-language">siSwati</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mpumalanga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Swazi-speaking communities also live in South Africa, especially in <b>Mpumalanga</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swazi-kingdom">Swazi Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
